onship that BLOOD is responsible for moving nutrients, making nutrient distribution a central physiological role in this community. [Data: Entities (904, 337); Relationships (5981)]\\n\\nNUTRIENT ABSORPTION is defined as the process by which nutrients are taken up for use by the body, and it is directly linked to NUTRIENTS in the relationships data. Together, these entities connect the digestive uptake step to the later bloodstream distribution step. [Data: Entities (1079, 904); Relationships (1674)]\\n\\nELECTROLYTES are described as helping regulate movement of nutrients into cells, adding a regulatory dimension that bridges “nutrients in blood” to “nutrients used by cells.” [Data: Entities (1757, 904)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"ELECTROLYTES provide cross-cutting physiological support, including nerve function\",\n      \"explanation\": \"ELECTROLYTES are characterized as charged ions in the blood that help regulate nutrient entry into cells and waste removal out of cells, and they are commonly monitored as part of routine blood chemistry testing to assess overall balance and function. This positions electrolytes as a clinically monitored control layer affecting multiple transport processes described elsewhere in the community. [Data: Entities (1757)]\\n\\nBeyond transport regulation, ELECTROLYTES are explicitly connected to NERVES via a relationship stating that electrolytes help nerves function properly. This creates an additional functional branch from the blood chemistry/transport axis into neurophysiology. [Data: Entities (1757); Relationships (2924)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Blood components (red blood cells and platelets) appear as key sub-entities within the blood-centric model\",\n      \"explanation\": \"RED BLOOD CELLS are identified as one of the main cellular components of BLOOD, with a central function of carrying oxygen throughout the body. This relationship situates oxygen transport as a major blood function alongside the